58. Secondary Data, Census and Sampling
Paper: Data Collection and Analysis for Social Science Research (ECNAEC301) | Unit: I | Topic: Primary and secondary data: meanings; characteristics of secondary data; census and sampling
Introduction
Data for research come from primary sources and secondary sources. The choice between collecting all units (census) or a part (sample) is also basic to research design.
Primary versus secondary data
• Primary data: collected afresh for the first time, by the researcher, for the problem at hand. Original, specific, but costly and slow.
• Secondary data: already collected by someone else for another purpose and used by the researcher. Examples: Census of India, NSSO/NSO surveys (PLFS, consumption expenditure), RBI Handbook of Statistics, Economic Survey, World Bank data, published reports, journals, company records.
Characteristics of secondary data
Advantages: cheap, quick, wide coverage, allow time-series and cross-country comparisons, and useful for background. Limitations: may not fit research objectives, may be outdated, different definitions and units, unknown accuracy, and potential bias.
Criteria before use (Bowley): suitability (objectives, definitions, units match), reliability (compiler's expertise, source, methods), adequacy (coverage, time period). Check for consistency and compare with other sources.
Census method
Enumeration of every unit in the population (Census of India every ten years; Economic Census).
• Merits: complete, accurate for each unit, good for small populations and detailed regional data, no sampling error.
• Demerits: costly, time-consuming, large staff, not feasible for destructive or infinite populations, non-sampling errors may be large.
Sampling method
Selecting a part of the population to infer about the whole.
• Merits: lower cost and time, faster results, greater depth, possibility of quality control, often better accuracy through trained staff.
• Demerits: sampling error, risk of bias, difficult if population is heterogeneous, requires expertise in design.
Types:
1. Probability (random) sampling: simple random, systematic, stratified, cluster, multi-stage (used by NSSO). Each unit has a known chance of selection, so sampling error can be measured.
2. Non-probability sampling: convenience, judgement (purposive), quota and snowball. Cheap but cannot estimate sampling error.
Sample size depends on variability, desired precision, confidence level and cost. Errors: sampling error versus non-sampling error (non-response, measurement, coverage).
When to use which
Census for small, homogeneous or legally required counts; sampling for large populations and time-constrained research.
Conclusion
Secondary data save time but need critical evaluation; census gives full coverage, while well-designed sampling provides reliable, economical estimates.